WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.868
Hi everyone, and welcome to
NCHC's hands-on AI Agent demo.

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:06.972
Today, with just one sentence,

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:12.302
an AI Agent runs the brain-image analysis
of fifteen MRI scans from epilepsy

00:00:12.302 --> 00:00:14.827
patients on the Nano4 supercomputer.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:17.272
First, some background.

00:00:17.272 --> 00:00:21.394
Before epilepsy surgery,
doctors run a pre-surgical evaluation:

00:00:21.394 --> 00:00:26.039
high-resolution brain MRI is registered
and analyzed to help locate the

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:27.283
epileptogenic zone.

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:33.115
The problem: on a hospital computer,
one scan takes twenty-four hours,

00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:35.555
so fifteen scans take fifteen days.

00:00:35.555 --> 00:00:38.205
NCHC's supercomputers are much faster,

00:00:38.205 --> 00:00:41.553
but every step still has
to be launched by hand.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.068
With an AI Agent, that changes completely.

00:00:45.068 --> 00:00:50.020
The fifteen scans are already uploaded to
Nano4, and I give just one instruction:

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.343
"Please run FreeSurfer on this batch."

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:58.329
The batch: fifteen brain MRI scans from
eight patients under epilepsy evaluation.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:03.295
The view sweeps upward through all eight
brains at once: brainstem, hippocampus,

00:01:03.295 --> 00:01:05.716
thalamus, all the way up to the cortex.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:10.890
The Agent first checks the environment
and confirms that all fifteen scans are

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:11.377
complete.

00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:15.384
Then it proposes a plan: the compute
resources and the estimated run time,

00:01:15.384 --> 00:01:16.954
and it waits for my approval.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.855
I reply: "Run them all at once."

00:01:19.855 --> 00:01:23.154
All fifteen go straight into
Slurm and start together.

00:01:23.154 --> 00:01:26.148
Then I add:
"Report progress every five minutes."

00:01:26.148 --> 00:01:28.714
And it schedules the check-ins on its own.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:33.643
First report: all fifteen are running,
doing intensity correction.

00:01:33.643 --> 00:01:38.341
Ten minutes in, skull stripping is done
and intensity normalization has begun.

00:01:38.341 --> 00:01:40.269
Every stage is reported clearly.

00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:44.903
About twenty-six minutes in,
we log in to Nano4 to take a look:

00:01:44.903 --> 00:01:49.273
all fifteen jobs are running, and all
of them are on the same compute node.

00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:50.484
Why?

00:01:50.484 --> 00:01:55.005
Each scan needs only a few CPU cores,
so all fifteen fit on one machine.

00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:55.821
On that node,

00:01:55.821 --> 00:02:00.719
FreeSurfer's brain-registration program
is running on dozens of cores at once.

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:02.943
Let's fast-forward.

00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:07.173
After a little over four hours,
only one scan is still running.

00:02:07.173 --> 00:02:09.926
At about six hours, all fifteen are done!

00:02:10.867 --> 00:02:14.710
The Agent then verifies,
converts and packages the results,

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.925
and delivers an acceptance report,

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:20.117
with download steps and
a way to check the files.

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:25.332
Following the Agent's guide,
I connect to Nano4 with FileZilla,

00:02:25.332 --> 00:02:30.679
open the results folder, and select all
fifteen zip files, about 3.8 gigabytes.

00:02:30.679 --> 00:02:34.267
After two-factor authentication,
the download starts.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.505
Now for the results!

00:02:36.505 --> 00:02:40.158
First, whole-brain segmentation:
for all eight patients,

00:02:40.158 --> 00:02:44.854
FreeSurfer automatically labels the
cortical and subcortical structures.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.295
Next, registration:
two scans of the same patient.

00:02:49.295 --> 00:02:52.999
Before registration,
the edges jump around; after it,

00:02:52.999 --> 00:02:58.311
they line up almost perfectly, and the
residual error drops by 84.6 percent.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:02.505
Then we screen the brain
regions linked to epilepsy.

00:03:02.505 --> 00:03:07.174
A coronal slice through the medial
temporal lobe highlights the hippocampal

00:03:07.174 --> 00:03:10.225
subfields, the amygdala,
and the thalamic nuclei.

00:03:10.225 --> 00:03:13.899
In both patients shown,
the left side is clearly atrophied.

00:03:14.833 --> 00:03:18.827
Lining up the left-right asymmetry
index for all eight patients,

00:03:18.827 --> 00:03:22.945
the red dashed lines mark the
plus-or-minus ten percent threshold.

00:03:22.945 --> 00:03:26.065
Structures beyond it are
where doctors look first.

00:03:26.065 --> 00:03:29.934
Remember: this supports interpretation;
it is not a diagnosis.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.189
To sum up: all fifteen
main analyses are complete,

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:38.795
each producing four core image files,
packaged into fifteen zip files.

00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:43.377
From a one-sentence request
to results you can verify:

00:03:43.377 --> 00:03:47.224
this is AI-Agent-driven research
computing on NCHC's HPC.

00:03:47.224 --> 00:03:50.126
Thanks for watching,
and see you next time!

